DSP options ported from jarredou's AudioSR Colab fork
(https://github.com/brahianrosswill/AudioSR-Colab-Fork — the same `inference.py`
that upstream merged through PR #73). They are re-implemented for the low-VRAM
pipeline in `run_lowvram.py` and exposed in both the CLI and the Web UI:

    --input_cutoff         resample the model input to `cutoff * 2` Hz instead
                           of low-pass filtering it (fork v0.5)
    --multiband_ensemble   keep the original audio below `cutoff - 1000` Hz and
                           mix in the model output above it (fork v0.3)
    --loudness_match       match every processed chunk to the LUFS loudness of
                           its input chunk instead of its peak, removing the
                           chunk-to-chunk volume jumps (fork v0.5)

All three default to OFF, so the stock pipeline and its output are unchanged.

Two further changes on top of that port:

    --synthetic_air        OFF by default. Mixes *synthesised* 2nd-order
                           harmonics into 24-43 kHz so a 96/192 kHz export is
                           not empty up top. This is generated content, NOT
                           recovered detail: AudioSR's mel stops at 24 kHz
                           (`fmax=24000`) and its VAE/vocoder are 48 kHz models,
                           so nothing above 24 kHz can be recovered. Upstream
                           issue #9 ("Going beyond 48KHz, up to 88KHz and
                           beyond") has been open since 2023 with no solution,
                           and the only published approach that goes beyond
                           48 kHz (AudioLBM, NeurIPS 2025 - cascaded latent
                           bridge models, any-to-192 kHz) has not released code
                           or weights. Every open-source audio-SR/BWE model found
                           (AudioSR, AERO, FlashSR, FLowHigh, AP-BWE, UniverSR,
                           FastWave, ClearerVoice-Studio, ...) tops out at
                           48 kHz.
    resampling             the `--out_sr` conversion and the fork input
                           band-limiting now use librosa (soxr when available)
                           instead of `torchaudio.functional.resample`. That
                           resampler uses a short Hann-windowed sinc whose
                           stop-band is roughly -15 dB just past the cutoff, so
                           exporting 96 kHz folded audible images of the
                           21-24 kHz band into 24-27 kHz, and input
                           band-limiting allowed about -70 dB of aliasing back
                           into the passband. The replacement rejects both by
                           100+ dB; the 8-48 kHz content itself is unchanged.

Not included in this repository: the Python virtual environment (`.venv/`),
the model weights (`pytorch_model.bin`), generated audio (`output*/`) and the
Web UI upload cache (`webui_uploads/`) — all excluded via `.gitignore`.

Two upstream files were modified on purpose:

    requirements.txt           the `git+https://github.com/huggingface/diffusers.git`
                               line is commented out. Nothing in this repository
                               imports `diffusers` (verified), and cloning from
                               GitHub inside pip frequently fails in CN networks
                               ("Recv failure: Connection was reset"), which
                               aborted the whole install. Re-enable it if you
                               actually need diffusers.
                               `pyloudnorm` was added for the optional LUFS
                               loudness matching (see below); the feature falls
                               back to peak matching when it is absent.
                               `soxr` was added as an optional faster backend
                               for librosa's high-quality resampling (see
                               below); librosa's own filter is used without it.

    audiosr/clap/training/data.py
                               `RobertaTokenizer.from_pretrained("roberta-base")`
                               used to run at import time, which pulled ~500 MB
                               from the Hub (or broke offline starts) even though
                               the AudioSR inference path never uses the CLAP text
                               tokenizer. It is now loaded lazily on first use.

Nothing else inside `audiosr/` (model architecture, training and inference
code) was changed.

